  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nThe shortwave moving through the area now will be the last of the\nweak waves we have seen over the past several days. Starting today\nwe will shift to a slightly different regime, however the chance \nfor showers and storms will stick around a little bit longer. \nLater today a lobe of energy will eject off of the upper level \nlow centered over the Hudson Bay. This will drift southwards into \nthe high plains giving most of the CWA a shot at precipitation \nthis evening into Tuesday. \n\nStorms will kick off of the mountains mid-to late afternoon just\nas that area of broad scale ascent moves into the region. Storms \nshould be able to initiate fairly easily in this region of CVA \ndue to the enhancement of the mountains as well as the lack of \nany CIN due to sufficient daytime heating. SB and ML CAPE values \nare both not anything to write home about, however widespread \n500-1000 J/Kg will be plenty to sustain these storms in \nconjunction with the modest 7-8 C/Km mid-level lapse rates. \nMoisture content should be fairly similar to today. The question \nhere will be whether or not the surface winds will be able to \nreturn to easterly early enough today to support decent moisture \nadvection prior to convective initiation. Regardless, it does \nlook like these storms will be decently high based which would \nresult in a downburst / gusty wind threat. The HREF ensemble is \nsuggesting 0-6 Km sheer may be widespread over 40 kts with \npockets of 50 kts. While I do think this may be a touch overdone,\nthis suggests that the environment will be supportive of isolated\nsupercell development amongst the scattered thunderstorms. The \nmain limiting factor in terms of hail development is the lack of \nCAPE and how skinny the profile is. That being said, the shear in \nthe hail growth zone is very supportive of tilted storms and \nhydrometer size sorting. In short, strong winds will be the main \nthreat this afternoon and evening with the potential for some \nlarge hail in the strongest storm or two. \n\nAfter the initial vort max, a couple more push through our region\nis quick succession Monday night through Tuesday evening which\nwill allow for continuous chances for showers and thunderstorms. \nWhile strong storms are less likely on Tuesday, the threat for \nheavy rain may go up as the atmosphere gets progressively more \nsaturated throughout the event. \n\nWednesday will be a transition day as the upper level low moves off\nto the east. Showers will probably be hanging around the area\nWednesday morning and gradually diminish / move east as the day\ngoes on. By Wednesday night a rige will be in place over the\nintermountain region which will keep most of the CWA warm and \ndry. As weak vort maxes ride along the ridge mid to late week, there\ncould be widely isolated showers in the eastern 2/3 of the CWA any\ngiven day. This pattern will continue into the weekend as temps \nslowly increase each day. There is still a difference between the \nGFS and ECMWF ensembles in terms of highs for this weekend as the\nGFS wants to have us flirt with triple digits again Saturday \nwhereas the ECMWF keeps highs in the low 90s.\n\nThe ridge should not stick around too long though as models hint\nat it only lasting into the weekend. The potential for a trough to\nmove off of the BC coast and track just north of the \ninternational border is possible. For anyone who is looking \nforward to the next season, this seems to have a hint of an Autumn\nflavor to it in terms of location and progression. WMR",
